WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.402
The lock manager has three jobs.

00:00:05.402 --> 00:00:08.803
You saw the first, grant or queue.

00:00:08.803 --> 00:00:12.205
Now the other two.

00:00:12.205 --> 00:00:15.607
The order it grants locks in is the microschedule, and that order decides both the speedup and the deadlock.

00:00:15.607 --> 00:00:19.017
Every transaction asks it for a lock.

00:00:19.017 --> 00:00:22.427
T1 wants row A.

00:00:22.427 --> 00:00:25.837
T2 wants row A.

00:00:25.837 --> 00:00:29.247
T3 wants row B.

00:00:29.247 --> 00:00:32.657
Each request goes to one component, the lock manager.

00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:36.067
The lock manager keeps the lock table.

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:39.477
It records who holds each row and who is queued behind them.

00:00:39.477 --> 00:00:42.887
Row A is held by T1, with T2 and T3 waiting.

00:00:42.887 --> 00:00:46.297
Row B is free, so it goes to T3.

00:00:46.297 --> 00:00:49.707
From that table it does three jobs.

00:00:49.707 --> 00:00:53.117
First, it grants or queues each request by checking compatibility.

00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:56.527
Second, the order it grants locks is the microschedule, the time-ordered record of who runs when.

00:00:56.527 --> 00:00:59.937
Third, it watches the WaitsFor graph, the running record of who is waiting on whom.

00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:03.347
Those three jobs are the rest of this page.

00:01:03.347 --> 00:01:10.847
The lock manager sees each transaction as atomic steps: request a lock, get it granted, read and write while holding it, then unlock.

00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:18.347
Lay every transaction's steps on one timeline, and you see who waits on whom.

00:01:18.347 --> 00:01:20.814
A read or a write takes time.

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:23.281
It starts, waits on the disk, then finishes.

00:01:23.281 --> 00:01:25.748
Watch one read on a row.

00:01:25.748 --> 00:01:28.215
Read-start here.

00:01:28.215 --> 00:01:30.683
Then a gap while the disk works.

00:01:30.683 --> 00:01:33.150
Read-end later.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.617
During that gap, the transaction is idle.

00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:38.084
Interleaving fills the gap with another transaction's work.

00:01:38.084 --> 00:01:41.537
The simplest schedule the lock manager can grant runs T2 first, then T1, then T3, one at a time, no overlap.

00:01:41.537 --> 00:01:44.991
Follow T2, the first to run.

00:01:44.991 --> 00:01:48.444
T2 requests the lock on A.

00:01:48.444 --> 00:01:51.898
The lock is granted, so T2 holds A.

00:01:51.898 --> 00:01:55.351
Read start: T2 reads the value.

00:01:55.351 --> 00:01:58.805
Read end.

00:01:58.805 --> 00:02:02.258
Then T2 unlocks A.

00:02:02.258 --> 00:02:05.712
Now T1 runs the same way, taking A next and adding a write between its read and its unlock.

00:02:05.712 --> 00:02:09.165
Then T3 runs last, on B and then A.

00:02:09.165 --> 00:02:12.619
End to end, forty-nine time units.

00:02:12.619 --> 00:02:16.072
That is the serial baseline the interleaved schedules have to beat.

00:02:16.072 --> 00:02:22.458
When transactions interleave, some have to wait, blocked on a lock another holds.

00:02:22.458 --> 00:02:28.843
The lock manager draws that as a graph: one node per transaction, an arrow from each waiter to the holder it needs.

00:02:28.843 --> 00:02:32.752
Now the lock manager interleaves the same three transactions.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:36.660
Completion drops from forty-nine units to thirty-one.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.569
The lock records track each lock: who requested it, who holds it, who is blocked.

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:44.478
T1 requested A early, but had to wait.

00:02:44.478 --> 00:02:48.386
T2 got A first, and held it.

00:02:48.386 --> 00:02:52.295
T3 took B, then reached for A.

00:02:52.295 --> 00:02:56.204
Now turn those records into a graph: one node per transaction.

00:02:56.204 --> 00:03:00.112
An edge runs from a blocked transaction to the one holding the lock it wants.

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:04.021
On the timeline, T1 held A until twenty-three, so T3 waited on T1.

00:03:04.021 --> 00:03:07.930
And T2 held A until ten, so T1, in turn, waited on T2.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.838
The result is a chain, not a cycle.

00:03:11.838 --> 00:03:15.747
Every transaction reaches the front and finishes.

00:03:15.747 --> 00:03:19.261
Interleaving is not automatically fast.

00:03:19.261 --> 00:03:22.775
Here is a different valid schedule of the same transactions.

00:03:22.775 --> 00:03:26.289
Same operations, different lock-acquisition order.

00:03:26.289 --> 00:03:29.803
This one finishes in forty-five units, barely better than serial.

00:03:29.803 --> 00:03:33.317
The waits are longer and the overlap is worse.

00:03:33.317 --> 00:03:36.831
The order the lock manager grants in matters as much as the decision to interleave.

00:03:36.831 --> 00:03:39.738
Until now the WaitsFor graph has been a chain, and everyone eventually moved.

00:03:39.738 --> 00:03:42.645
Here it closes.

00:03:42.645 --> 00:03:45.552
T1 holds the lock on A and asks for B.

00:03:45.552 --> 00:03:48.459
T2 holds B and asks for A.

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:51.367
T1 waits for T2, T2 waits for T1.

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:54.274
The two arrows form a cycle.

00:03:54.274 --> 00:03:57.181
Neither can move, neither will ever release, and the system is stuck.

00:03:57.181 --> 00:04:00.088
That cycle has a name.

00:04:00.088 --> 00:04:02.995
A deadlock, drawn in red.

00:04:02.995 --> 00:04:05.902
The lock manager breaks it by aborting a victim.

00:04:05.902 --> 00:04:10.137
So when is interleaving worth the risk?

00:04:10.137 --> 00:04:14.373
Almost always.

00:04:14.373 --> 00:04:18.608
The bigger the disk wait and the more transactions in flight, the bigger the win.

00:04:18.608 --> 00:04:22.844
With one hundred transactions and a long IO lag, the lock manager finishes about twenty-four times faster than serial.

00:04:22.844 --> 00:04:27.079
You manage the deadlock risk because the speedup is enormous.

00:04:27.079 --> 00:04:29.896
The lock manager grants locks, and the order is the microschedule.

00:04:29.896 --> 00:04:32.714
The WaitsFor graph asks one thing.

00:04:32.714 --> 00:04:35.531
Is there a cycle?

00:04:35.531 --> 00:04:38.348
A cycle is a deadlock, broken by aborting a victim.

00:04:38.348 --> 00:04:41.166
Next, a strategy where readers never wait.

00:04:41.166 --> 00:04:43.983
MVCC.
